  The PI-lower-than-PD ordering is Joos's own and the model must reproduce
  the SIGN of it: a fuller ocean buffers less, so a pulse lasts longer.

=== 5. PULSES ON FUTURE BACKGROUNDS (100 GtC in 2050, COUPLED climate) ===
scenario   CO2@2050   T@2100   IRF@20   IRF@50  IRF@100 | int IRF @100
ssp126          438     1.76    0.539    0.373    0.281 |         42.4
ssp245          471     2.62    0.571    0.411    0.307 |         45.5
ssp370          501     3.50    0.596    0.457    0.355 |         49.5
ssp585          521     4.41    0.619    0.494    0.375 |         52.3

  Joos (Bern3D-LPJ, Fig. 5): IRF_CO2 falls to ~70 % within two decades in
  EVERY scenario, then keeps falling for RCP2.6 and the 389 ppm standard but
  turns back UP for the higher pathways as CO2 and warming continue to rise;
  for RCP8.5 the airborne fraction of the pulse is still above 80 % at year
  1000. THE ORDERING IS THE TESTABLE CLAIM HERE, NOT THE LEVEL -- a model
  calibrated to the historical record is out of sample in these futures, and
  Marcus made exactly that caveat when asking for the comparison.
